INT. REQUISITIONED RAILWAY SIGNAL BOX - NIGHT

A narrow room of levers and dials, one dim lamp. Rain on the glass. Down the line, a ruined station smolders. A telegraph key sits silent on a shelf.

OBERLEUTNANT KASPAR WEIDENFELD-ABARA (50s, frostbitten ear, an officer's greatcoat open) sits at a small table, writing a letter. Across the room, a captured soldier, PRIVATE DESMOND OKORO-HALVERSEN (20s, hands tied loose, a bandaged shoulder), watches.

Kaspar writes. Desmond watches. A clock ticks.

KASPAR
You may speak.

DESMOND
I've nothing to say.

KASPAR
Then you may be quiet.

Kaspar continues writing. The pen scratches. Desmond looks at the lever frame.

DESMOND
Are you writing home?

KASPAR
I am.

DESMOND
Does she know where you are?

KASPAR
She knows I am cold.

Desmond almost smiles. He catches himself.

DESMOND
My mother would ask if I was eating.

KASPAR
Are you?

DESMOND
Not for two days.

Kaspar sets down the pen. Reaches into his coat. Brings out a heel of black bread. Breaks it in half. Holds out one half.

Desmond doesn't move.

KASPAR (CONT'D)
It is not poisoned.

DESMOND
I know.

KASPAR
Then eat.

Desmond leans forward. Takes it. Chews. Neither speaks. The rain softens.

DESMOND
Thank you.

KASPAR
It is only bread.

DESMOND
(quietly)
It's not.

Kaspar nods, once. He returns to his letter. After a moment he stops.

KASPAR
What is your mother's name?

DESMOND
Why?

KASPAR
So I know who to write to.

DESMOND
Don't write to her.

^KASPAR
Don't write to her.

Both men stop. A long beat. Desmond drops his gaze. Kaspar folds the letter and puts it in his coat.

DESMOND
It was Ruth.

KASPAR
Ruth.

DESMOND
Ruth Okoro.

Kaspar says the name again, softly, to himself, and then gets up, crosses to the window, and watches the smoke on the horizon curl and fade.

KASPAR
It is a good name.

DESMOND
It was.

Kaspar does not turn around. The clock ticks. The telegraph key, for the first time all night, begins to chatter.

They both look at it. Neither moves.

KASPAR
That will be the order.

DESMOND
What order?

KASPAR
The one that decides which of us goes home.

The key chatters on. Kaspar picks up the receiver. Listens. His face is a closed door.

He puts it down.

KASPAR (CONT'D)
(very quietly)
It is over.

DESMOND
What is?

KASPAR
All of it.

Desmond stares at him. Rain on the glass. Kaspar crosses the room and kneels in front of the young man. He takes a knife from his belt. Desmond flinches. Kaspar cuts the rope at his wrists.

KASPAR (CONT'D)
Go and tell your mother.

Desmond rubs his wrists. He doesn't move.

DESMOND
And you?

KASPAR
I will finish my letter.
